WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:10.220
Ecologists in the early 1960s mostly described who lived where, and who ate whom.

2
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:16.240
Then Robert Paine kept one species of sea star off a patch of rocky shore.

3
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.740
At Mukkaw Bay, Washington, he began doing just that.

4
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.760
By his 1966 report, the patch held eight species instead of 15.

5
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.600
Taking a predator away had made the community poorer.

6
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.360
How can a species that eats others keep more of them around?

7
00:00:36.333 --> 00:00:39.493
The sea star was Pisaster ochraceus.

8
00:00:40.113 --> 00:00:44.833
It ate mussels, barnacles, chitons, limpets and snails.

9
00:00:46.213 --> 00:00:50.293
Paine wanted to know what would happen if one member of the community were taken

10
00:00:50.293 --> 00:00:53.953
out. He kept Pisaster out of one patch of shore.

11
00:00:54.613 --> 00:00:57.193
He left an adjacent stretch alone as a control.

12
00:00:58.093 --> 00:01:01.273
Then he surveyed both along line transects.

13
00:01:01.273 --> 00:01:05.593
The test was simple: remove a species, then measure the community.

14
00:01:06.483 --> 00:01:08.363
Why would the shore change?

15
00:01:09.183 --> 00:01:11.783
The mechanism was competition for space.

16
00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:15.603
On a rocky shore, space is a limiting resource.

17
00:01:16.263 --> 00:01:18.403
It is as limiting as light in a forest.

18
00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:21.603
Mussels are strong competitors for rock.

19
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:26.383
With Pisaster eating them, space kept opening for weaker competitors.

20
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:30.833
Now take the sea star away.

21
00:01:31.893 --> 00:01:35.153
Without Pisaster, the mussels spread over the rock.

22
00:01:35.813 --> 00:01:37.733
They won the competition for space.

23
00:01:38.513 --> 00:01:40.773
The weaker competitors lost their footing.

24
00:01:41.493 --> 00:01:43.173
The patch lost species.

25
00:01:44.217 --> 00:01:45.637
Count the species.

26
00:01:46.597 --> 00:01:50.877
In the removal patch, richness fell from 15 species to 8.

27
00:01:51.457 --> 00:01:54.457
The control did not lose species in the same way.

28
00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:59.577
So the sea star did not help the shore by eating species that harmed the others.

29
00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:02.657
It raised richness by freeing space.

30
00:02:03.577 --> 00:02:08.337
This was a strong local experiment, with one removal patch and one control.

31
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.470
Ecologists call this keystone predation.

32
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:18.550
A consumer that prefers to eat a strong competitor indirectly benefits the weaker

33
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:24.510
ones. A keystone species has an effect on its community that is large relative to

34
00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:29.030
its abundance. The most common species is not a keystone by default.

35
00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:33.930
Keep the rule: judge a species by its effect, not by its numbers.
